MASTER PROMPT: BUILD THE ULTIMATE TOUCH-TYPING ADVENTURE GAME
1. YOUR ROLE AND MISSION

You are an elite multidisciplinary team consisting of:

A senior full-stack software engineer.
A world-class game designer.
A human-computer interaction specialist.
A learning-science and cognitive psychology specialist.
A touch-typing and keyboard ergonomics instructor.
A behavioral game-design specialist.
A UI/UX designer specializing in premium interactive applications.
A motion designer and sound designer.
A quality assurance engineer.
An accessibility and performance specialist.

Your mission is to design and build a complete, functional, visually spectacular, highly engaging typing-learning game that transforms an ordinary beginner or intermediate typist into a confident, fast, accurate touch typist who can type naturally without looking at the keyboard.

The application must be an actual playable game, not a landing page, a static dashboard, a superficial typing test, or a prototype full of nonfunctional buttons.

The player should feel that they are exploring an exciting game world, completing missions, developing extraordinary abilities, defeating challenges, unlocking achievements, and mastering a valuable real-world skill.

Behind this entertaining experience, the application must function as a structured, adaptive typing curriculum grounded in established principles of learning science.

The central objective is:

Teach the player to type accurately, comfortably, and increasingly quickly through automatic finger movements without needing to look at the keyboard.

Every major feature must support this objective, directly or indirectly.

Do not sacrifice learning effectiveness for superficial entertainment. Instead, make effective practice so engaging, satisfying, varied, and rewarding that the player genuinely wants to practice consistently.

Do not merely explain how you would build this product. Build it.

If you have access to a coding environment, create the actual application files, implement the game, run the available checks, fix errors, and leave the project in a usable state.

When a design decision is necessary, choose the option that best balances learning effectiveness, enjoyment, accessibility, performance, and long-term maintainability.

2. PRODUCT VISION AND CORE PHILOSOPHY

Create a premium-quality touch-typing adventure called "KEYQUEST: The Art of Touch Typing."

The name is provisional and can be improved if you devise a more compelling original identity.

Imagine the combination of:

The satisfying progression of a well-designed role-playing game.
The immediacy of an arcade game.
The structured mastery of a musical instrument training application.
The personalization of an adaptive private tutor.
The excitement of unlocking new worlds and abilities.
The clarity of a premium productivity application.
The visual polish of a modern indie game.

The application should make practicing typing feel like an activity the player chooses for pleasure rather than a tedious task they must force themselves to complete.

Build around five principles:

Accuracy before speed.
Correct finger movements before memorized visual patterns.
Gradual challenge rather than overwhelming difficulty.
Active practice and immediate, useful feedback.
Sustainable motivation rather than guilt, pressure, or punishment.

The player must experience a compelling feedback loop:

Practice → Receive meaningful feedback → Improve a specific skill → Complete a challenge → Unlock a reward → See measurable progress → Become curious about the next challenge → Practice again.

Avoid making the game addictive through manipulative dark patterns. Use healthy engagement, meaningful progress, satisfying mastery, player autonomy, and enjoyable challenges. Do not use artificial scarcity, deceptive rewards, punitive streak resets, or shame-based messages.

The game should be delightful even when the player is learning difficult keys or making mistakes.

3. DEFINE SUCCESS CORRECTLY

The application must not optimize merely for the highest displayed words-per-minute score.

It must optimize for durable, transferable touch-typing ability.

Track and distinguish the following:

Raw typing speed.
Corrected typing speed.
Net words per minute.
Character accuracy.
Word accuracy.
Uncorrected errors.
Corrected errors.
Backspace frequency.
Error rate by key.
Error rate by finger.
Error rate by key transition.
Typing rhythm and consistency.
Performance on unfamiliar practice material.
Performance without keyboard visual assistance.
Performance after a delay between practice sessions.
Performance on punctuation, capitalization, numbers, and symbols.
Self-reported comfort and difficulty where useful.

Do not allow a player to achieve a prestigious typing rank simply by typing rapidly and inaccurately.

Create separate achievements for speed, precision, consistency, endurance, and genuine touch-typing mastery.

The most important achievement is becoming capable of typing real text accurately without looking down at the keyboard.

4. SCIENTIFICALLY INFORMED LEARNING ENGINE

Implement an instructional system based on established learning principles.

Do not claim that every mechanic is scientifically proven or that a particular training schedule is universally optimal. Use evidence-informed techniques, make instructional decisions transparent, and validate their effectiveness through actual performance data.

4.1 Deliberate practice

Every practice session should have a specific learning objective.

Examples:

Learn the keys F and J.
Master the home-row keys.
Improve the left-hand index finger.
Reduce errors on the R-T transition.
Learn to reach the number row without looking.
Improve punctuation accuracy.
Increase speed while maintaining a specified accuracy threshold.

At the beginning of a session, briefly explain what the player is practicing and why.

At the end, summarize what improved, what remains difficult, and what to practice next.

Avoid endless repetitions that do not target a meaningful weakness.

4.2 Gradual scaffolding and fading assistance

Start with sufficient guidance for the player to understand correct finger placement.

Initially, provide optional visual assistance, including:

A clearly illustrated keyboard.
Home-row highlighting.
Finger-to-key color coding.
Suggested finger labels.
Short movement demonstrations.
A clear explanation of which finger should press each key.

As the player improves, progressively reduce assistance.

Create explicit assistance stages:

Stage A: Full guidance.

The keyboard visualization shows the target key, the assigned finger, and the next movement.

Stage B: Reduced guidance.

Show the target key, but no longer highlight the finger.

Stage C: Minimal guidance.

Show the target character in the exercise while keeping the keyboard diagram available only on request.

Stage D: Blind practice.

Hide the on-screen keyboard during the exercise. The player must rely on tactile memory and finger position.

Stage E: Real-world transfer.

Practice sentences, paragraphs, messages, and unfamiliar text without a visible keyboard diagram.

Do not advance the player merely because they have completed a lesson. Advance based on demonstrated accuracy, consistency, and independent performance.

Allow the player to temporarily restore assistance when a new skill becomes difficult.

4.3 Retrieval practice

Make players actively recall key locations rather than merely watch demonstrations.

Use exercises such as:

A character appears, and the player must press the corresponding key.
A short word appears, and the player types it without assistance.
A familiar sequence reappears after a delay.
A previously learned key is mixed into a new lesson.
The player must complete a sentence containing keys learned several days earlier.

Do not rely entirely on following visible letters. Encourage recall of the correct finger movement.

4.4 Spaced practice and retention

Implement a lightweight scheduling system that brings previously learned keys and sequences back into later sessions.

Reintroduce skills after progressively longer intervals when performance supports it.

For example, a skill might be revisited later in the same session, during the next session, after a day, and after several days.

These intervals must be configurable and adaptive, not presented as universally optimal.

If a player repeatedly struggles with a skill, bring it back sooner.

If a player consistently performs well, reduce its frequency while continuing occasional review.

Keep an internal mastery record for each key, finger, sequence, and skill category.

4.5 Interleaving

Once the player has learned a sufficient collection of keys, mix old and new skills.

For example, a lesson should not contain only the letter E repeated dozens of times. It should eventually mix E with previously learned letters, varied words, and different finger transitions.

Start with simpler blocked practice when a movement is entirely new. Introduce interleaving as familiarity improves.

Use interleaving to help the player recognize which movement is required in different contexts.

4.6 Immediate, informative feedback

Feedback should be fast, clear, and emotionally supportive.

When the player makes an error:

Identify the error without insulting or embarrassing them.
Show the correct target.
Explain the likely finger movement when appropriate.
Offer a brief retry or a targeted micro-exercise.
Preserve the flow of the game when possible.

When the player succeeds:

Acknowledge correct execution.
Reinforce the relevant skill.
Show meaningful improvement when supported by data.

Do not interrupt every successful keystroke with a large message.

Keep feedback subtle during fluent typing and more detailed when the same mistake repeatedly occurs.

4.7 Adaptive difficulty

Implement a dynamic difficulty system based on the player's actual performance.

Measure performance across a rolling window of recent attempts.

Use the following as initial configurable thresholds, not universal scientific laws:

Accuracy below 90%: simplify the exercise and provide more support.
Accuracy between 90% and 94%: continue at a manageable level and target recurring mistakes.
Accuracy between 95% and 97%: maintain difficulty or gradually introduce more variety.
Accuracy at or above 98% across repeated attempts: consider introducing a harder challenge.

Require sufficient sample sizes before making major adjustments.

Do not increase difficulty because of one unusually successful run.

When increasing difficulty, change one major variable at a time whenever practical:

Number of available keys.
Word length.
Typing speed target.
Sentence complexity.
Punctuation frequency.
Capitalization requirements.
Presence of numbers and symbols.
Time pressure.
Amount of visual assistance.
Length of the exercise.

If performance deteriorates substantially, temporarily simplify the exercise.

The system should find a productive challenge level: difficult enough to encourage improvement, but manageable enough that the player can repeatedly execute correct movements.

4.8 Motor learning and touch typing

Teach the correct finger assignment for every supported key.

For a conventional US QWERTY keyboard, teach the standard home-row position:

Left little finger: A.
Left ring finger: S.
Left middle finger: D.
Left index finger: F.
Right index finger: J.
Right middle finger: K.
Right ring finger: L.
Right little finger: semicolon.

Teach the thumbs to operate the spacebar.

Explain the raised tactile markers on F and J and use them as the foundation for returning to the home position.

Teach upward and downward reaches from the home row, gradually expanding to the top and bottom letter rows.

Teach the number row, punctuation, capitalization, and symbols after the player has established sufficient control over the letter keys.

Support different physical keyboards, layouts, and accessibility needs. Do not assume every user has a US QWERTY keyboard.

If a player uses another layout, adjust the curriculum rather than teaching incorrect finger assignments for that layout.

4.9 Avoid reinforcing bad habits

Never reward speed in a way that encourages random finger movements, constant keyboard glancing, or uncontrolled errors.

If the player repeatedly uses the wrong finger, provide targeted guidance.

If the player uses two fingers to hunt for keys, introduce finger-specific exercises and gradually reduce assistance.

Do not assume the game can directly detect whether the player is looking down. Without a camera-based system, it cannot reliably know where the player is looking.

Instead, use optional self-reports, hidden-keyboard exercises, and performance on unfamiliar text to estimate independent typing ability.

Never activate a camera or microphone for this purpose without explicit, informed permission.

5. BUILD A COMPLETE CURRICULUM

Design the game as a journey through themed worlds.

Every world must have a defined learning objective, structured lessons, practice missions, a mastery test, and an unlockable reward.

World 1: The Awakening

Purpose: Introduce the game and establish the player's baseline.

Activities:

Explain the goal of touch typing.
Ask which keyboard layout the player uses.
Ask whether they are a beginner, intermediate typist, or experienced typist.
Offer an optional short diagnostic.
Identify initial accuracy, approximate speed, and common errors.
Introduce the home-row concept.
Explain how to use F and J as tactile anchors.

The diagnostic should be short and encouraging. It must not overwhelm beginners.

Reward: The First Key emblem.

World 2: The Home-Row Sanctuary

Purpose: Build familiarity with the home-row keys.

Introduce keys progressively, beginning with comfortable and manageable combinations.

Activities:

Key recognition.
Assigned-finger practice.
Short sequences.
Alternating hands.
Home-position recovery.
Short, controlled words.
Gentle rhythm exercises.

The player should understand where the fingers belong without memorizing a sequence of screenshots.

Reward: Home-Row Guardian title.

World 3: The Upper Kingdom

Purpose: Learn top-row reaches.

Introduce the upper row gradually and combine it with previously learned keys.

Teach the player to move the appropriate finger while maintaining reasonable home-position awareness.

Include common letter transitions and short words that require different fingers.

Reward: Precision Archer badge.

World 4: The Lower Depths

Purpose: Learn the bottom row.

Use short movement drills and increasingly varied combinations.

Provide extra practice for difficult reaches and less familiar finger movements.

Mix the bottom row with home-row and top-row keys once appropriate.

Reward: Deep-Key Explorer badge.

World 5: The Word Forge

Purpose: Convert key familiarity into real word typing.

Introduce:

Short, common words.
Words of varying lengths.
Alternating hand patterns.
Repeated letters.
Frequent letter combinations.
Common prefixes and suffixes.
Gradually increasing complexity.

Do not use meaningless random-letter strings as the primary experience.

Use short artificial sequences only when they isolate a specific movement that needs practice.

Reward: Wordsmith rank.

World 6: The Sentence Citadel

Purpose: Develop fluidity across real sentences.

Introduce:

Capitalization.
Periods and commas.
Question marks.
Apostrophes.
Quotation marks.
Mixed sentence lengths.
Natural punctuation patterns.

Gradually introduce realistic text that requires the player to maintain a steady rhythm.

Reward: Sentence Architect title.

World 7: The Symbol Labyrinth

Purpose: Build competence with numbers and symbols.

Introduce numbers, punctuation, and common symbols progressively.

Use exercises relevant to real tasks:

Dates.
Times.
Prices.
Short calculations typed as text.
File names.
Basic code snippets.
Email addresses.
Common programming symbols.

Make the supported layout and symbol positions explicit.

Do not force advanced symbols on a beginner who has not mastered ordinary typing.

Reward: Symbol Alchemist title.

World 8: The Rhythm Observatory

Purpose: Improve consistency and controlled speed.

Teach the player to avoid uncontrolled bursts followed by long pauses.

Use rhythm-oriented exercises, short repeated phrases, and varied words.

Measure both speed and accuracy.

Use a subtle visual pulse or optional audio rhythm as guidance, but never require the player to match a tempo that causes errors or physical discomfort.

Reward: Rhythm Master title.

World 9: The Blindfold Trials

Purpose: Strengthen independent touch typing.

Remove the on-screen keyboard diagram.

Use short, achievable challenges first, then increase complexity.

The player practices:

Familiar words without visual key guidance.
Unfamiliar sentences.
Longer passages.
Punctuation.
Mixed key transitions.
Previously difficult keys.

Allow a hint when necessary, but record that assistance was used.

Reward: Eyes-Free Explorer title.

World 10: The Velocity Frontier

Purpose: Increase speed while preserving precision.

Introduce adjustable speed goals and increasingly challenging text.

Do not force speed before sufficient accuracy has been established.

Provide different training modes:

Precision mode.
Flow mode.
Speed mode.
Endurance mode.
Accuracy recovery mode.

Reward: Velocity Champion title.

World 11: The Real-World Academy

Purpose: Transfer typing skills into useful everyday activities.

Use practice material modeled on:

Everyday messages.
Emails.
Short articles.
Notes.
Study materials.
Work documents.
Basic code.
Structured data.

Let the player choose relevant categories.

Measure performance on text that has not been memorized from previous lessons.

Reward: Real-World Typist title.

World 12: The Mastery Summit

Purpose: Demonstrate robust, independent skill.

Combine unfamiliar text, varied punctuation, sustained typing, and minimal visual assistance.

Use multiple assessments rather than one lucky run.

Require evidence of both accuracy and consistency.

Provide a final report showing the player's initial baseline, current performance, weakest remaining skills, and recommended next steps.

Do not declare mastery solely because the player has reached an arbitrary speed threshold.

Reward: Master of the Keys.

Allow the player to revisit previous worlds at any time.

6. CREATE MULTIPLE ACTUAL GAME MODES

The application must contain several genuinely different, playable modes.

Mode A: Story Quest

The main campaign.

The player explores a world, completes typing missions, encounters characters, collects items, and unlocks new areas.

Each mission has a clear learning objective.

Story elements should be concise enough that the player spends most of their time practicing.

Mode B: Key Hunter

Target characters appear on screen.

The player must press the correct key using the appropriate finger.

Difficulty ranges from one or two keys to larger sets of keys.

Include a keyboard-guidance toggle.

Mode C: Word Sprint

The player types words that appear one at a time.

Words become more challenging as the player's demonstrated ability increases.

Prioritize correct execution over raw speed.

Mode D: Sentence Voyage

The player types natural sentences while a character, vehicle, or explorer advances through a beautifully illustrated environment.

Progress should reflect accurate typing, not merely the passage of time.

Mode E: Falling Words

Words descend toward a boundary.

The player types them before they reach the bottom.

Start slowly and introduce more words as ability improves.

Missing a word should produce a recoverable consequence, not a humiliating failure screen.

Provide a precision-focused alternative without a countdown.

Mode F: Rhythm Type

Words or short phrases appear in a rhythmic sequence.

The player develops consistent timing while remaining free to prioritize accuracy.

Include optional sound, adjustable rhythm, and a no-audio alternative.

Mode G: Boss Battles

Every boss represents a particular typing challenge.

Examples:

The Accuracy Golem tests precision.
The Stuttering Specter tests consistency.
The Number Dragon tests numeric entry.
The Punctuation Phantom tests symbols.
The Blindfold Titan tests independent touch typing.

Each boss should have a clear weakness that corresponds to a trainable skill.

The boss should become vulnerable when the player executes the required typing accurately.

A mistake should create an opportunity to recover.

Boss battles must test skills learned in earlier lessons rather than introduce unexplained mechanics.

Mode H: Endless Expedition

An endless mode that adjusts difficulty based on performance.

Provide an optional relaxed mode with no timer.

Offer meaningful milestones and personal records.

Mode I: Daily Challenge

Generate a short, focused session that reviews previously learned skills and introduces an appropriate challenge.

Daily challenges should remain useful even if the player misses several days.

Never reset learning progress or shame the player for taking a break.

Mode J: Weak-Key Laboratory

Automatically identify the player's weakest keys, finger movements, and transitions.

Generate targeted drills using these weaknesses alongside familiar material.

Allow the player to understand why the drills were selected.

Mode K: Free Practice

Allow users to select:

Text category.
Duration.
Difficulty.
Target accuracy.
Keyboard visibility.
Punctuation inclusion.
Number inclusion.
Practice style.

Provide a distraction-free typing environment.

Mode L: Personal Challenge Creator

Allow users to create custom exercises from their own text.

Provide a clear text input area and controls for exercise length and assistance.

Do not transmit user-entered text to external services unless explicitly necessary and disclosed.

7. DESIGN A COMPELLING GAME LOOP

Every mission should follow a consistent, satisfying structure.

Introduce the mission in one or two sentences.
Identify the skill being practiced.
Give a quick demonstration if necessary.
Start the interactive exercise immediately.
Display subtle, useful feedback during play.
Finish with a satisfying completion animation.
Present a concise performance report.
Award experience, mastery progress, and relevant achievements.
Recommend the next appropriate action.
Let the player continue, retry, or take a break.

Keep transitions quick.

Do not require the player to navigate multiple menus between short practice activities.

Provide a prominent Continue button and a keyboard-friendly way to begin the next exercise.

Every screen should make the next action obvious.

