12. BUILD A PRECISE TYPING ENGINE

The typing engine must be correct, responsive, and thoroughly tested.

Do not implement typing as a simple animation that advances whenever a key is pressed.

Implement real character-by-character comparison.

Input handling

Support physical keyboard input and appropriate browser input methods.

Handle:

Letters.
Numbers.
Spaces.
Punctuation.
Shift-modified characters.
Capitalization.
Backspace.
Enter where appropriate.
Keyboard layouts.
Input focus.
Restarting and pausing.
Accidental repeated input.
Browser shortcut conflicts where safely manageable.

Do not capture keystrokes when the player is typing into a settings field or another unrelated input.

Keep keyboard handling scoped to the active game.

Do not globally prevent all keyboard shortcuts.

Use semantic controls and predictable keyboard navigation.

Error handling

Choose and document a consistent error model.

Support configurable modes:

Strict mode: the player must correct mistakes before continuing.
Flow mode: the player can continue, but errors are recorded.
Free mode: corrections are unrestricted and the goal is practice rather than scoring.

For every mode, make the scoring rules clear.

Track corrected and uncorrected mistakes separately.

Do not silently erase evidence of errors when a player presses Backspace.

At the same time, do not punish correction so severely that players avoid learning to correct mistakes.

Text comparison

Implement accurate comparison between expected and actual input.

Handle Unicode carefully.

Distinguish character-level accuracy from word-level accuracy.

Be explicit about whether whitespace and punctuation count toward scoring.

Avoid hidden rules that make scores inconsistent.

Timing

Use a reliable monotonic timing source for session duration where available.

Exclude paused time from active typing time.

Handle tab changes and window focus appropriately.

Do not let a background tab produce unrealistic speed results.

Scoring metrics

Calculate and label these metrics consistently:

Raw WPM = correctly entered or entered characters according to the documented metric, divided by five, divided by active minutes.

Net WPM = net correct characters divided by five, divided by active minutes.

Use a consistent convention for spaces, punctuation, and corrected mistakes.

Because typing applications use different definitions, display the exact convention in the methodology or help screen.

Accuracy = correct character attempts divided by total character attempts, multiplied by 100.

Define how corrected errors affect the metric and keep that definition consistent.

Do not display an impressive speed score when the sample is too short to support a meaningful estimate.

Use minimum sample sizes and clearly identify short-sample results as provisional.

Session summary

At the end of each exercise, show:

Net WPM.
Raw WPM where useful.
Character accuracy.
Total errors.
Corrected errors.
Most difficult keys.
Most difficult transitions.
Comparison with recent comparable exercises.
Whether accuracy improved, remained stable, or declined.
A suggested next exercise.

Use honest comparisons.

Compare like with like whenever possible: similar text complexity, duration, and assistance level.

13. MAKE BLIND TYPING A FIRST-CLASS FEATURE

This feature is central to the entire product.

Create a dedicated "Eyes-Free Training" system.

The player should progressively learn to type without looking at an on-screen keyboard diagram.

Phase 1: Learn the tactile anchors

Teach the F and J home-row markers.

Encourage the player to locate them through touch.

Phase 2: Practice returning home

Create exercises that ask the player to reach a key and then return to the home position.

Explain the concept of a stable home-row reference.

Phase 3: Hide the keyboard diagram

Allow the player to type known sequences without visible finger guidance.

Keep the target text readable.

Phase 4: Introduce unseen words

Use words the player has not repeatedly memorized.

Measure performance without the keyboard diagram.

Phase 5: Blind sentence challenges

Introduce natural sentences and realistic punctuation.

Phase 6: Independent typing assessment

Ask the player to type a new paragraph without on-screen keyboard assistance.

Evaluate accuracy, speed, consistency, and performance on unfamiliar material.

Phase 7: Retention challenge

After an appropriate delay, assess the same underlying skills using different text.

Do not require the player to remember a specific passage.

Assistance transparency

Clearly record whether keyboard assistance was visible.

Never claim to know whether the player looked down at a physical keyboard.

If the player voluntarily reports looking down, use that information to suggest more suitable exercises without punishment.

Make the player feel proud of gradually becoming independent.

14. CREATE SHORT, FUN, HIGH-VARIETY PRACTICE

Design sessions that fit naturally into everyday life.

Offer suggested session lengths such as:

2 minutes: Quick Quest.
5 minutes: Daily Training.
10 minutes: Skill Expedition.
15 minutes: Deep Practice.
20 minutes: Extended Challenge.

Allow longer sessions, but do not encourage users to continue when their hands are tired or uncomfortable.

Vary the exercise format while keeping the learning objective coherent.

For example, a five-minute session could include:

A brief warm-up.
A targeted weak-key drill.
A short game challenge.
A previously learned skill review.
A tiny end-of-session assessment.

Do not fill every session with random minigames.

The variety should support learning rather than interrupt it.

Provide an option to practice only one skill for players who prefer focused repetition.

15. IMPLEMENT ACCESSIBILITY AND PHYSICAL COMFORT

Typing should feel sustainable.

Include:

Adjustable text size.
High-contrast mode.
Colorblind-friendly feedback.
Reduced-motion mode.
Adjustable sound.
Full keyboard navigation.
Screen-reader-friendly labels.
Clear focus indicators.
Configurable animation intensity.
Optional dyslexia-friendly font settings where feasible.
A no-timer mode.
A low-pressure practice mode.

Do not rely on color alone to communicate correctness.

Avoid rapid flashing.

Avoid visual effects that obscure the current typing target.

Use accessible contrast ratios.

Provide concise reminders to relax the hands and take breaks during longer sessions.

Do not make unsupported medical claims about posture or ergonomics.

If a player reports discomfort, suggest pausing or stopping rather than pushing through pain.

Do not use punitive countdowns as the only way to play.

