19. CONTENT QUALITY AND EXERCISE GENERATION

Create a substantial initial library of exercises.

Do not populate the game with only a handful of repetitive examples.

Include varied, carefully designed content for each curriculum stage.

Beginner content

Use short sequences, familiar words, and simple combinations that only require already-introduced keys.

Intermediate content

Use varied words, transitions between fingers, short sentences, and previously learned punctuation.

Advanced content

Use realistic paragraphs, unfamiliar text, more complex punctuation, numbers, and varied sentence structures.

Content rules
Do not repeat the same sentence excessively.
Avoid nonsensical content unless it serves a specific motor-learning purpose.
Keep exercise length appropriate to the player's level.
Ensure generated words do not accidentally require unavailable keys in restricted beginner exercises.
Make target text easy to read.
Avoid offensive or disturbing text.
Provide multiple content categories.
Allow the player to skip a topic they do not want to practice.
Clearly identify exercises that focus on a specific key or movement.
Distinguish memorized practice from unseen transfer practice.

Where possible, build a reusable content generator instead of manually writing every possible exercise.

For each exercise, record its intended learning objective and the skills it trains.

